In article <2gf22n$aki@golem.wcc.govt.nz>, hamilton_b@ix.wcc.govt.nz writes:
|> In article <CJ61uo.60D@cs.uiuc.edu>, kadie@cs.uiuc.edu (Carl M Kadie) writes:
|> >hamilton_b@ix.wcc.govt.nz writes:
|> [...]
|> >Most US libraries, especially academic ones, offer interlibrary loans
|> >to anyone, even a reader in New Zealand.
|> 
|> Not True, Interlibrary loans _are_ between Libraries, and the
|> rules of the borrowing Library still apply!. As far as I'm aware
|> US libraries do not lend to overseas individuals, they suggest we
|> contact our nearest library.

In most US library systems, the reader requests an interlibrary loan for a book
his own library does not have available. Here in the US, where citizens are
credited with enough intelligence and responsibility to choose their own reading
material, interlibrary loans are materials loaned by an institution to a person
in another location. The access rules for the remote library are exactly the same
as those for the local library.
|> 
|> >Most US libraries, as a matter of policy, do not put age or
|> >restrictions on library cards. The idea is libraries should not act
|> >_in loco parentis_. (By extension, do not count on any American
|> >library act _in loco governmentis_ (sp?), collaborating with the
|> >censorship policy of other countries.)
|> 
|> Sorry, you will be aware that adult and restricted material despatched 
|> to most countries _is_ confiscated at the border and destroyed. It
|> is not returned to the lending institution.  I doubt US libraries
|> send copies of Satanic Verses on request to Middle East readers.

I doubt it too. Interlibary loans are made only between libraries that belong in
the same interlibrary loan system. I do get books from abroad through
interlibrary loan, and I am willing to stake fourteen gallons of fine brown
whiskey that books coming on loan from the US libraries to member libraries
abroad ARE NOT confiscated. It would cause an international incident if it were
to happen. Americans take their rights rather seriously, and the US government
does its best to protect those rights of its citizens even in international
circimstances. It doesn't always make us popular. But we do it anyway.
|> |> 
|> >I enclosing the American Library Association policy on challenged
|> >materials and on childern's access to nonprint items.
|> 
|> Note, this is the Association policy, which may or may not be
|> adhered to as closely as governments adhere to pre-election
|> policies.

We adhere to it, bub, or we find ourselves in a court of law trying to explain
why we thought we had the right to deny an American citizen complete and open
freedom of access to information. The Association is supported by the full weight
of the Constitution of the United States of America, and the association is held
to that standard. Folks from other countries often fail to understand that this
is a serious, nearly a sacred, matter in the US. 


|>  New Zealand ( like some other countries ) has a government-
|> appointed Censor, who censors books, films, videos using criteria
|> that are frequently debated. It is unlikely any NZ Library would
|> violate that legal censorship, nor should they, as most of the
|> population are not sufficiently concerned about negative consequences
|> of censorship to request change. We could not export our policy.

I couldn't agree more.
|> 

|> countries did ). Perceptions change according to the period, but
|> in the case of Fanny Hill, the _archive_ administrator is only
|> acting according to his perception of the the University policies.
|> He is not censoring, he has decided that Fanny Hill is not
|> appropriate for his archives, also knowing that it is available
|> elsewhere, and he is not interfering with that availability.
|>  
|> The refusal of Cica to hold Fanny Hill is their choice, and to
|> interpret that as censorship is quixotic.

Bruce. You just don't get it. This fellow is representing a public institution.
Whether that public institution is aware that it is being represented by him is
in doubt. Of course he has the right to remove Fanny Hill if he decides he thinks
it is inappropriate. Many Americans would willingly go to war to defend his right
to do so. However, he SAID he was doing it in the name of a state-supported,
state-regulated, state-run institution of higher learning. If THOSE folks own and
run his archive, then he has to observe the laws associated with FREEDOM OF
INFORMATION. In our country that means NO CENSORSHIP. Sorry. You don't know what
your are talking about, nor to whom you speak.  

Also, many of us from Wyoming think our sheep and trout are superior to yours
too, though I will admit that Mt. Cook is one of my favorites. Right up there
with Gannet. :>
