Derived from a column distributed by the Newspaper Enterprise 
Association as printed in the 10 JAN 94 Rocky Mountain News.

Original column written by Nat Hentoff "a nationally recognized
authority on the First Amendment and the Bill of Rights."

The sequence of events:

- The University of California (Riverside California USA) branch
  of the fraternity Phi Kappa Sigma creates/distributes a tshirt
  that depicts a man "wearing a serape and a sombrero, and holds
  a bottle."  The front of an over-the-Mexican-border bar "much
  frequented by American college students" is also shown.

  Circling the drawing are the words from a Bob Marley "antiracist
  song"; " "It doesn't matter where you come from, as long as you
  know where you are going." "

- MEChA (the Movimiento Estudiantil Chicano de Aztlan) charges the
  shirt " "dehumanizes - and promotes racist views of - Mexican
  people." " as well as being the model of " "fighting words" "
  under the university's code of community values.

- Rafael Escribano, an ass't prof of Spanish defends the shirt,
  making note that "this fraternity counts within its ranks
  plenty of members from non-Caucasion backgrounds..."

- The school administration removed the frat's charter, and then
  suspended the frat from the campus for three years.

- John Howard, president of the Individual Rights Foundation in
  Los Angeles (California USA) filed suit assisted by the "new
  section of the California Education Code which says the regents
  of the University of California "shall not make or enforce any
  rule subjecting any student to disciplinary action solely on the
  basis of conduct that is speech or other communication which,
  when engaged in outside the campus, is protected from governmental
  restrictions by the First Amendment of the United States Consti-
  tution or the California Constitution." "

- Howard then proposed a settlement, and in light of the defense
  costs of a battle they would probably lose, the administration
  agreed to not only to the restoration of the fraternity to the
  campus but the members responsible for the "court martial" of the
  frat had to attend " "First Amendment sensitivity training." "

The column ends with the following comment from Nat Hentoff:

"Indications are that getting college officials to brush up on the
First Amendment may spread to other University of California
campuses.  But why not high school principals and other admini-
strators, too?  And certainly school boards."

