From caf-talk Caf Jan 11 11:43:09 1994
From: morgan@engr.uky.edu (Wes Morgan)
Newsgroups: alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk
Subject: Re: A (positive) example of student archives
Date: 11 Jan 1994 16:39:54 GMT
Message-ID: <2gukoq$805@s.ms.uky.edu>

In article <CJG8vF.Hy3@cs.uiuc.edu>, Carl M Kadie <kadie@cs.uiuc.edu> wrote:
>Students in the College of Engineering at the U. of Illinois
>(Urbana-Champaign) can set up their own WWW pages. 

Neat!

Hmm...what would be the bureaucratic/ethical/administrative questions in
applying the same technique to anonymous FTP archives?  I'd like to be
able to allow my student (and faculty) users to include some of their
directories in an anonymous FTP archive.   Here's the sort of thing I'm 
envisioning, portrayed as a hypothetical ftp session:

ftp> cd /users
ftp> dir
drwxr-xr-x  19 morgan   users       3584 Jan 10 17:20 morgan -> /usr/morgan/ftp
drwxr-xr-x   2 smith	users        512 Oct 29 15:03 smith  -> /usr/smith/ftp
drwxr-xr-x   2 jones    users        512 Sep 24 15:16 jones  -> /usr/jones/ftp
drwxr-xr-x   2 bill     users        512 Dec  9 13:16 bill   -> /usr/bill/ftp
drwxr-xr-x   3 lisa     users        512 Sep 28  1992 lisa   -> /usr/lisa/ftp
drwxr-xr-x   7 stan     users        512 Oct 22 08:18 stan   -> /usr/stan/ftp
ftp> quit

[ For those of you unfamiliar with Unix, the notation "foo -> /usr/foo/ftp" ]
[ indicates that the ftp "users/foo" directory is not physically a part of  ]
[ the "/users" directory; it's actually a directory named "ftp" over in     ]
[ /usr/foo.  This is usually called a _symbolic link_.                      ]

A few notes:

	- None of these are "official" ftp archives.  I envision placing
	  the "officially supported" material (which would be formally
	  screened/vetted/blessed) in the traditional /pub directory.

	- A README file in the top-level ftp directory would inform the
	  user that "files in /pub are officially supported; files in /users
	  are placed and maintained there by indidivual users." 

	- None of these are system directories; each is merely a link into
	  each user's private diskspace.  Therefore, all files count against
	  each user's individual resource quota.   This also, in my opinion,
	  offers these files a certain protection as "user files," as opposed
	  to "files in an institutional archive."

	- Users would not require institutional review/permission before
	  making files available in the anonymous ftp archive; they'd sim-
	  ply move the files into their own "ftp" subdirectory.  (I'd write
	  a shell script to do the initial setup for new ftp users, so they
	  wouldn't have to contact me to set it up.)

It would seem to me that this sort of setup would neatly sidestep the issue
of "institutional control," since these are user files in user diskspace; I
would take the position that any questions/comments/complaints should be ta-
ken directly to the individual user.

Please feel free to poke gaping holes in any or all of my assumptions in
this matter; I'd like to test this theory severely before discussing any
implementation.

--Wes

