>> evidence presented feeds the anti-academia climate based on distortion and
>> hyperbole.
> I apologize for the apparent lack of clarity of my posting.  I did
> not intend to equate "esoteric" with "frivolous" in any way.
>> The course may, in fact, be frivolous. But it's arrogant to make
>> such a claim without seeing the course objectives and reading list.
> Agreed; when I said "esoteric," I was using its definitions as "intended for
> or understood only by a particular group" and "Confined to a small group."

You know, when I did undergraduate physics I spent a long time doing an
experiment on the gravitational attraction of small lead balls.  Who the
hell needs to know how small lead balls gravitate?  It's not as if this
is something anybody can make productive use of, is it?  An effect that
after 200 years of trying can only be observed as a second-order wobble on
a wobble measured over hours using the deflection of a light beam?  That
must only appeal to a *really* tiny group...

Well, obviously not.  It illustrates universal gravitation and gives you a
way to measure the gravitational constant.  It's the easiest way of looking
at a physical phenomenon of literally cosmic importance as it operates on a
small scale.  Think you could persuade a redneck politician trying to cut
education spending of that?  Probably not.

Unusual social situations can be used in the same way in social research.
What is the effect of people's perceptions of their own gender on their
relationship to the world?  How do you control for the fact that nearly
everybody's gender and biological sex match?  Look at a large population
and there's no way you could identify any effect due to this subjective
factor.  Find an experimental population where the two are discordant -
transsexuals - and you have a way of isolating an effect you otherwise
couldn't study.  Now maybe attempting to understand the causal relationships
between sexuality, the mind and society *is* an esoteric minority interest,
but the minority involved isn't transsexuals, it's anybody with an empirical
curiosity about the social world.

Now *that* particular group really *is* threatening to the right-wing agenda,
hence the censorship efforts we've seen here.

