decent news client and just go to an alternate newsserver site. That way,
the restriction will be moot, and the University can continue to stick
their heads in the sand. From my discussions with senior adminstrators here
so far, free speech is a non-issue, one entirely not worth fighting for at
the risk of "legal ramifications". Free speech is dead and the tyranny of
law is our new Big Brother apparently. I am hopeful however since the SLIP
service will allow better, quicker, more effective access to the Internet
for our users and then the "cover-our-ass" decisions like the policy in
place will be of no consequence. Anyways, the letter is enclosed. Feel free
to post this writing as well as the enclosures anywhere that may be of use.
And thanks for your help to date with research on these issues.

----------- begin letter to VP Academic of UVic, BC Canada -------
Dear Mr. Scully,

Thank you for your reply to my last inquiry regarding what I thought was a
ban on the Alt.* newsgroups. I understand that there is no ban in place but
that the groups are being withheld from the University members unless they
have received written permission based on "academic grounds".

I have not however been able to find written details on it. Also, I don't
understand the motivation or reason for this policy to be in place in a
University setting.

I apologize for the length of this letter, but there are several important
issues raised by this policy that need to be addressed.

The University:

o may be acting in a legally dangerous manner with respect to its liability for
  the content of the Internet;
o may be contributing to a net INCREASE in computing resource use on this campus
  by forcing users to use alternate methods to access the same information;
o is promoting an even larger gap than currently exists between the computer
  literate and illiterate by inhibiting those without computer knowledge from
  accessing an alternative source of information, discussion, dialogue, and
  support for their various academic and personal pursuits;
o is restricting access (by the computer illiterate only) to global
  communication and the free exchange of ideas and information with people from
  all over the world.
o is maintaining a policy which is technically impossible from a network
  perspective;
o is inhibiting some (computer-illiterate) members of the University community
  from associating with support groups such as those for abortion, adoption,
  homo- or bi-sexuality, or child support by forcing them to get permission to
  connect to various informationa and support newsgroups.

POTENTIALLY INCREASED LEGAL LIABILITY
-------------------------------------

q: Does a University reduce its likely liability by screening Netnews
for offensive articles and newsgroups?

a: Not necessarily. By screening articles and newsgroups the
University may *increase* its liability.

In the United States, "if administrators exercise the power of prior review
over for example a student publication, then the court will also hold them
and their schools liable for the contents of such publications." (excerpted
>from the CAF FAQ on liability for USENET news - source available if you
wish). This policy may in fact be opening up the University to claims of
liability in obscenity, copyright, or sexual harassment issues.

I realize that American and Canadian law sometimes vastly differ. It is for
this reason that I would like to know if the University's legal counsel has
ever researched this question. As Canada tends to be behind on these
issues, I would doubt that it has been brought up. If it is still
ambiguous, and the University cannot clearly be held liable under current
law, then these groups should be unrestricted again since it the goal of
any University (and hopefully this one as well) should be to obtain access
to as much information as it possibly can in support of its research
community. If liability was in fact determined by legal counsel, won't the
University still be held liable for images or other obscene or copyright
infringing materials which are brought across the network by any means?
What if the material uses your network, but is delivered to a student's
home computer?

(As an aside, it is my understanding that both provincially and federally
there are attempts being made to grant common carrier status to all
Internet style data communications in order to alleviate questions of
liability for content. This would put the University clearly in the same
arena as the telco which is not legally responsible for the content of any
voice transmission even though the voice is travelling through their
equipment.)

SEXUAL HARASSMENT, COMPUTERS, AND UNIVERSITIES
----------------------------------------------

Over the last few years, many universities have temporarily or permanently
stopped distributing certain newsgroups at campuses around North America
and Europe. The targets are usually alt.sex.bondage,
alt.binaries.pictures.erotica, and similar groups. The usual genesis of the
restriction is a sexual harassment complaint or pressure from an interest
group.

As I understand it, the current policy was instituted after a sexual
harassment case here at the University. Regardless of whether or not this
case took place here, many other universities have had to go through
similar times. Those which stood up firmly for the Charter of Rights and
Freedoms (or in the U.S. the First Amendment), reinstated the newsgroups
immediately. Others banned them, justifying the ban by saying that some of
the groups, since they were being read at school, created a "hostile"
environment. Other schools have network news policies which forbid their
systems people to restrict access without going through the same process
required to have a book removed from a library. In other words, network
news and library collections are granted similar status.

>From what I have heard so far, it appears to me that the current policy was
implemented in order to stop students from retrieving and displaying
sexually explicit materials. The logic was that if the materials were
unavailable, then the University couldn't possibly be named in any future
suit. I hope that this was not the reasoning at this University, but I know
it has been used at several others.

If legal counsel has already been approached about these issues, what did
they have to say with regards to liability in sexual harassment cases. Has
the owner of a computer screen or the owner of an offensive book ever been
legally liable for the actions of another person using the materials to
harass another individual? Sexual harassment committees are designed to
protect students and staff from other PEOPLE, not from the tools or
implements they use. These committees serve a very worthwhile purpose but
for them to attack the owner of the tools and not the agent is
irresponsible. And for a university to withdraw resources or otherwise
restrict access to them because of their "potential" as tools for sexual
harassment is also irresponsible. Following that line of reasoning, access
to all VCR's (they can play illegal porno tapes and movies which have been
copied from the television), books, bulletin boards, magazines, and phones
should also be restricted in a similar fashion as with newsgroups.

If this line of reasoning is the one being followed in this case, it sets a
very dangerous precedent. In the current nightmarish politically correct
environment, free expression and free speech are being curtailed by
activities such as speech codes and network restrictions. Administrations
who have control over some of these issues don't feel the support behind
them to speak up for RIGHT to free and open expression. The result is
usually sexual harassment codes with phrases like "all students have the
right to work in an environment which is not hostile." This type of wording
is outrageous, dangerous, undefinable, and very likely unconstitutional. It
allows a rude computer display, a rude t-shirt, and a foul mouth to be
considered "causes" of a "hostile environment". The Administration must
stand up against sexual harassment but they must also stand up for "freedom
of thought, belief, opinion and expression, including freedom of the press
and other media of communication" as granted in Section 2(b) of the Charter
of Rights And Freedoms. In the Constitution of Canada, there is no "Right
To Not Be Offended". Indeed, offense is a necessary or at least likely
occurrence in any free society. As any reasonable person would realize,
granting such a right would be the end of other rights.


RESOURCE USE ISSUE
------------------

If this policy was put in place because of resource use concerns, then
perhaps some calculations were not taken into account. Firstly, there is no
savings in storage space, since all of the groups still must be maintained
in order to serve news to those with access and also to pass the news
further on to those groups or schools which are "downstream" of us.
Secondly, since users can not get at the news in the regular manner, they
use alternate tools like e-mail, gopher, wais, mosaic, etc. in order to
download the articles from the alt.* groups which means there may be a net
increase in network resource use because of this policy. If the students
are saving the information on their home machines of course, there is no
net increase/decrease in cost to the University. 


ACCESS TO INFORMATION ISSUES
----------------------------

Because of this policy, the University is contributing fuel to that fire
which is the increasing gap between the computer literate and illiterate --
an unconscionable act in a University environment. Due to the
"hyperconnectedness" of the Internet, this policy can be simply ignored by
those with computer knowledge. They point their news programs at computers
overseas or in the United States instead, or use other tools as discussed
above. Meanwhile, those with little computer knowledge are deprived of a
source of immediate information, support, and discussion with students,
faculty, business people and researchers around the world on issues as
diverse as:

        abortion, abuse recovery, adoption support, agriculture, animation,
        archery, architecture, automobiles, atheism, bbs's, backrubs, binaries
        (software, pictures, sounds), books, business, CAD/CAM, censorship,
        child support, College Bowl football, comics, computers, consciousness,
        conspiracy, cults, culture, current events (front-line reports from the
        Gulf War were here and currently there are forums for news from Bosnia-
        Herzegrovina), cyberpunk culture, dad's rights, discrimination, dreams,
         drugs, education for the disabled, evil, fashion, feminism, folklore, 
        games, guitars, hacker-culture, hemp, homosexual activism and support,
        humour, hypertext, images (medical), individualism, journalism,
        programming languages, law enforcement, locksmiths, magic, meditation,
        military, missing-kids, mother-goose nursery rhymes, music, native
        issues, Food Not Bombs, parapsychology, personal ads, politics, prisons,
        privacy, psychoactives, psychology, quotations, rap music, religion,
        revisionism, rock and roll, romance, satanism, satellite tv, science,
        security, sex (including sexuality and sexual fetishes), skate-boarding,
        smoking, snowmobiles, societies, sports, support groups (for arthritis,
        cancer, dieting, etc.), surfing, surrealism, sustainable agriculture,
        tasteless jokes, thrash culture, television, visa, war,  and weddings.

I can think of no logical reason for a policy which restricts access to an
alternative and up-to-date source of information and dialogue on the above
subjects. Besides myself, there are many people on this campus that don't
think that censorship of this sort should be practiced simply because it
can be. In a University which is actively involved in promoting greater
understanding with its various exchange programs and which theoretically
upholds principles of academic freedom, open discussion, and equality of
access to information, this policy is totally out of place. As for the
reference to censorship, I do not use that term lightly. I am specifically
referring to the American Library Association's statement of definition of
censorship: "The change in the access status of material, made by a
governing authority or its representatives. Such changes include:exclusion,
restriction, removal, or age/grade level changes."

Based on this definition which is widely accepted, this current restriction
is censorship in its purest form. The only thing which is unclear is the
reason behind it.


TECHNICALLY IMPOSSIBLE POLICY
-----------------------------

As I understand, the news server on this campus like many others is
incapable of testing whether or not some_one_ connecting is authorized. The
main computer can only verify the name of the machine connecting. This
means that if my home PC or Mac is authorized, then anybody using it has
access to the newsserver. PCs and Macs do not require user identification
in order to function.


WHAT IS "ACADEMIC MERIT"?
-------------------------------------

The policy currently requires that any student wanting access to the alt.*
groups must first provide proof of academic merit to a senior adminstrator,
since computing equipment is to be used for university-type work ONLY. If a
student wants to borrow a book (also a scarce resource at this university)
>from the library which is about their hobby, let's say windsurfing for
example, they do not have to give proof that the book is required for
"university-type work". If a club would like to hold a meeting on campus,
they can borrow a room. This is also not "academic". Or is it? I would say
that University is a time and place for physical, intellectual, spiritual,
and social growth for all of its members. Our current system of library
use, rec centre use, promotion of health and fitness, our programs for
sexual education, and our willingness to invite controversial speakers to
come to this campus to discuss what might be for some students
"non-academically" related topics all reflect this. But for some reason,
computer use (even though it may be increasing the users familiarity with
the computer and allowing them to explore some other area of interest) has
somehow fallen outside of those principles of openness and exploration.
This is disappointing. What is the justification for this?


UNNECESSARY INVASION OF PRIVACY OF UNIVERSITY COMMUNITY MEMBERS
---------------------------------------------------------------

Some of the topics in the alt.* groups are such that anyone subscribing to
them would want to do so without having to obtain prior permission because
of the nature of the group. Some examples would be associating with support
groups such as those for abortion, adoption, homo- or bi-sexuality, or
child support. The desire to be involved in such support groups and forums
is outweighed by the fear of reprisal or embarassment which could be caused
by releasing this information to a senior Administrator who has no right to
know this information.



In summary, I would like to pose several questions to you and would
appreciate an honest reply.

1. Why are the alt.* groups being censored? What is the purpose of this policy?
2. Will we soon need the same kind of permission to use library facilities?
   (This is not a facetious question, I am very serious.)
3. Will we have to prove "academic merit" in order to take out books from the
   library? 
4. Was legal counsel ever consulted about this? If so, on what issues
   specifically? On what specific legal precedents and laws were the
   restrictions put in place if any.
5. Who was consulted before restricting these groups or was the decision made
   without any campus consultation?

Based on the answers to these questions, I hope that you will reconsider
fully the implications of the current policy and open access to all
students without discrimination and without any sort of bureaucratic
"clearing" or "approval" process.

I understand that you are incredibly busy at this time of year, as we all
are, but would appreciate hearing from you or someone in your office as
soon as possible with answers to my specific questions. If there is
somebody else who is able to answer these questions on your behalf, please
let me know so that I might contact them directly. Thanks in advance for
your patience and your time.



Sincerely,

Troy Angrignon.
