Subject:  an interesting twist on sexual harassment
Date: 12 Jan 1994 17:15:30 -0500
Message-ID: <9401102033.AA18827@tzone.mpd.tandem.com>

In the February issue of _Playboy_, there is an article about a professor
who thought that most studies of sexual harassment were insufficiently
scientific, i.e., they would ask women "Have you been sexually harassed?"
but would not define what exactly that means.  He set out to do a more
rigorously scientific survey.

There were several conclusions, but the one that I found most interesting
was this.  Giving two scenarios that were identical in every way save one,
about five times as many women identified the first scenario as harassment
than did the second one.

The one variable that was changed?  In the second scenario, the man was
physically attractive.  The study showed that this made the biggest difference
in whether or not women considered the man's behavior to be harassment.

I thought this was very interesting, as it highlights something that I have
long suspected:  "unwanted sexual activity", that bogeyman that men, as a
group, are supposed to avoid, is highly variable.  And not only from woman
to woman, but from man to man, as well.

Daniel Welch



