<5gb4sloanj@vmsf.csd.mu.edu> wrote:
>Why disclaimers?  I feel we've reached the point when such things should 
>just be understood.  I am sick of reading gutless disclaimers.  Are we free 
>to say what we want?  Obviously, we are not free.  If we were free, we'd 
>be acting like it.  

Here's a message I recently received:

(Paraphrased in respect of the author's confidentiality)

>>To: root@engr.uky.edu
>>
>>This posting was recently sent by a user at your site.  I hope that the
>>racism and bigotry seen here is not being taught at your university.  I
>>hope that you do something about this user and his racist talk.

When admins stop getting messages such as this, disclaimers can go away.

What message generated this little gem?  Well, it was one of my postings
to rec.sport.basketball.college, in which I took issue with the proposed
boycott of NCAA basketball games by the Black Coaches Association.  Read
the articles; with the exception of this one person, no one has found my
comments to be racist.  (If you do find them racist, please let me know)

In another recent case, a few complainants skipped the system admins and 
delivered their complaints, in email, to several high-level officials of
this university.  Since the postings involved were not mine, I am not at 
liberty to go into detail. (I was able to convince the administration to 
ignore the complaints and accept the notion that computer-mediated commu-
nications forums should be treated as areas for free expression.)

When people stop trying to silence opposing (or offensive) opinions, dis-
claimers may be retired.  Until that time, some will continue to use them
as a form of (limited) protection against the "mail the admins" crowd.

--Wes

